\chapter{Top-level functions and recursion}
\label{ch:10}
\index{Top-level functions and recursion}

A top-level \OnxCodeTerm{fn} declaration supplies a parameter list, a body, and optionally an explicit return type. Functions that return a value need a valid return on the relevant paths. A statement-only entry point can omit a value result. Recursive calls use the same source signature, and explicit annotations make the intended arithmetic and result paths easy to inspect.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn sum_to(n: i64) -> i64
{
  if n <= 0 { return 0; }
  return n + sum_to(n - 1);
}

fn main()
{
  assert(sum_to(6) == 21);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Parser nesting budgets concern how much input structure the compiler accepts. Runtime recursion uses the execution environment's call stack and is a different limit. To bound stack usage, rewrite this accumulator as a range loop and separately account for integer overflow. For inferred functions, add a result annotation at the first ambiguous call boundary before attributing a failure to recursive control. \cref{ch:14} covers type holes and elaboration.

\section{Keep track of unfinished additions}
Trace the call with input 3 using two columns on paper: the current
argument and the work waiting for a result. At 3 the waiting operation
is addition of 3; at 2 it is addition of 2 with the earlier addition
still pending. At 1 there are three additions pending. The call with
zero returns immediately, so the results traveling back up the chain
are 0, 1, 3 and finally 6. The recursive call occurs inside an addition;
it would be incorrect to jump straight to the next invocation and forget
all the waiting work.

To replace those waiting operations with data, maintain a running total
and the next positive integer to include. Before each iteration, their
meaning should be that the total plus the sum of the remaining integers
equals the original answer. Adding the next integer to the total and
decreasing it by one preserves this statement. When no positive integer
remains, return the total. Check input zero and a negative input as well
as 3: the original helper returns zero immediately for both nonpositive
cases. This check catches a rewrite that executes its loop body once
before testing the condition. The argument for equal sums applies only
while the arithmetic stays representable in the chosen integer type.

